WEBVTT

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:04.400
In outer space, there isn't an absolute
left, right, up, or down.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.940
It all depends on your relative position.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.690
Understand where you've come from
and where you're going,

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.070
which way you're facing,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.030
and you'll know your current position.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:19.450
Confronted by the vastness of space,

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:23.750
you may be disoriented by
how small you are.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.710
But overcoming that feeling

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.790
is your first step in outer space.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.050
Are you scared?

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:30.920
Yes.

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.340
However, I'm looking forward to it.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.530
So what's this "situation" we're facing?

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:20.870
Well, it isn't an emergency.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:24.080
So I thought "situation" would
be a better word for it.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.750
Tell me what's going on.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.710
The masts are fully extended

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.960
and the antennas are up,

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.000
so I ordered an omni-directional
scan to test our electronics.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.970
We picked up 3 spaceships

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:39.880
with unresponsive transponders.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.510
They're not asteroids or comets, correct?

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.350
Yes, we've checked.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.480
Is this supposed to be a
civilian ship's radar?

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.730
It isn't a navy flagship.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.900
Why would it need an IFF system?

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.110
Amazing, huh?

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.450
You said it.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.370
This ship's been around for a long time.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.660
It's a survivor of the Original Seven.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.040
Hmm...

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.420
Right now, we aren't seeing any ships
with transponders turned off.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.420
I think they're still in the logs.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.550
Of the three non-responsive ships,

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:20.300
one passed by at a high speed.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.550
The other two eventually disappeared.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:24.600
Disappeared?

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.810
One is the Bentenmaru.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.560
A pro got detected by a high school girl?

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:33.520
That's not a good sign...

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.520
Well, I'll let them know.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.440
About the other two, also.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.530
That's right...

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.660
Make sure to keep the
infirmary clean, Ms. Misa.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:46.200
I'll get around to it.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:06.470
Lower our turn speed by an
additional five percent.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:07.720
Roger!

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.360
The Odette II is on course

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.740
for the inner planets of
the Tau Ceti system.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.080
It's taking its time though.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:31.450
Yeah.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.540
It's a sailing ship.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.500
And since we don't need
to worry about fuel,

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.170
we might as well take our time.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.460
Right.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:46.340
Night.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.300
Good night.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.810
It really feels deliberate.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.980
Pairing the two of us on duty again...

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:57.650
Mr. Kane knows what he's doing.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.980
It isn't worth thinking about.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.610
Anyway, how does the radar scan look?

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:03.400
Huh?

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.700
This area...

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.160
There are fewer spaceships around
than when we left port, right?

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:10.660
Um...

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.750
The device that broadcasts your ship's

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.790
name, registration, present position,

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:16.830
and heading is called a transponder.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.340
And all ships in space are required

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.340
to keep theirs activated at all times.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.050
Why are you reading me
the navigation laws?

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.100
Currently, fifteen vessels
appear on our radar.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.640
Two of them are solar energy plants.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.390
One's an unmanned supply ship.

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.100
The remaining twelve

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.270
seem to have both IDs and
flight plans that check out,

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.900
I guess.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.030
<i>If</i> everyone is abiding by the law.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:48.160
The weather report from the relay station
is cutting out at regular intervals.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.160
What?

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.160
From the past logs,

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:55.160
periodic transmissions to the control bureau are persistently cutting out as well.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.670
It's unlikely that ground-based control

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.250
and the relay station would
cut out simultaneously.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:05.130
Maybe mysterious aliens have attacked and

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.050
are destroying my home planet.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.430
Yeah, I guess not.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.890
So maybe it's being jammed?

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.430
Or could it be the Lightning 11 again?

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.140
They're almost certainly hiding
somewhere nearby.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:23.080
Then should we check all the ships
on the radar individually?

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:24.160
That's a good idea.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:25.400
Let's get started.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.150
Right!

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:28.860
They're a good combination.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:32.660
What's the point of jamming
our communications?

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:34.080
What do you think?

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:38.540
If we were in trouble,
we couldn't send an SOS.

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:39.920
And...

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:44.170
They're probably collecting
data as they jam

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:45.630
our periodic transmissions.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.970
If we issue an SOS,

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.720
and they replaced it with,
"Odette II, all clear,"

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:54.140
we'd be done for.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:55.680
That's right.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.850
The Odette II's course circles
Tau Ceti and returns.

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:01.650
That's no secret.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.900
Anyone accessing the relay station
would know that.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.280
So if they wanted to try
something, they'd do it

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.820
on the other side of the star,
where people are less likely to see,

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:16.450
as we approach Sand of the Red Star.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.120
Maybe.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.750
That makes sense.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:21.790
Thanks.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:24.090
But you're forgetting
something important.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.090
What?

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:27.960
Do you plan to fight alone,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.170
on a ship with everyone on board?

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.390
You were amazing on that dinghy simulation.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.140
You also have good instincts.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:39.390
But if you act upon your instincts,

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:41.100
you'll involve everyone else.

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:48.570
To begin with, does anyone know

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:51.530
you're thinking about
captaining a pirate ship?

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:55.370
Around here, space pirates
are all but forgotten.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.740
If you told them, would they
even believe you?

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.460
Canceling the database update.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.000
Automatic scans show nothing.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:08.550
Changing electronic warfare
systems to manual.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.210
What are you going to do?

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.100
If we're this far apart,
we'll be okay, right?

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.560
What are you planning?

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:23.730
Even if we scan with the fire control
system's high-frequency radar,

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.020
they won't assume they're the
target at this distance.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.360
Are you serious?

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.650
Any nearby battleships will think
you're readying an attack,

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.530
and attack us without hesitation.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.860
That's why I scanned first.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.160
No battleships.

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:37.910
And it'd be their fault for hiding.

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:44.620
Set the radar for a close-range,
high-detail scan.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:46.630
At least that gives us a pretext.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:49.500
Roger!

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:52.800
Initiating omni-directional,
close-range, high-detail scan.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:55.970
Start!

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:00.810
Found them.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:03.430
500,000 km behind us.

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:04.310
A battleship?

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:06.810
The Pleiades-class battleship Alcyon,

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.360
belonging to the Colony
Federation Military.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.480
A battleship?

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:12.030
Are they faking their transponder?

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.990
This is the radar scan result.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:16.860
There's no transponder signal.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.280
It's too late for excuses now.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:21.990
One more!

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:29.130
No readings?

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:30.520
They got away?

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.420
Nothing on infrared either.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.880
And no gravity waves or energy signals.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:35.840
One more!

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:42.220
It's not there.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:43.520
That's impossible.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:44.980
It's in the logs.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:46.310
But...

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.650
The Pleiades-class battleship Alcyon,

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:51.440
of the Colony Federation Military...

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:53.570
It went down 120 years ago?

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.240
But the destroyed ship is missing

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.570
and no bodies were ever recovered.

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:01.330
Why would a ship like that...?

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:04.750
A ghost ship?

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:06.370
A ghost ship?

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:11.540
That's a rare thing to run into.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:13.800
It's <i>more</i> than rare!

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:18.090
So, what makes you think
it was a ghost ship?

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.550
Um...

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.720
It looked like our transmissions
were being blocked,

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.010
so I booted up the high-frequency radar.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.730
And the reading we saw on
the first omni-directional scan

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:30.190
was identified as a battleship.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:32.480
But that ship supposedly went down

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.030
during the wars for independence
120 years ago.

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:38.400
And when we looked into it,

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.320
several people had recorded seeing it.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:42.160
So...

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.910
Well, you needn't worry.

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:48.830
Lots of sailors

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:53.420
have had encounters with ghost ships.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:54.670
Have you?

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:58.090
Have you ever encountered one before?

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.590
Who knows?

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:03.550
Don't worry.

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:05.390
For the past hundred years,

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:09.140
no one's ever been downed by a ghost ship.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:11.770
Breakfast beats worrying.

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:13.020
Report to the cafeteria.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:20.030
This is Kane, on board the Odette II.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:22.490
Bentenmaru, can you hear me?

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:25.950
Well?

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.910
Mr. Kane is speaking to
someone outside the ship.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:29.540
To whom?

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.630
It's a secret, encrypted line.

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:35.500
So we're lucky that the ID code
was still listed in the phone book.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.060
The space pirate ship: Bentenmaru?

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:41.840
Pirate ships are listed in the phone book?

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.680
It's one of the benefits of
being sanctioned.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:45.720
They <i>do</i> have licenses after all.

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:46.850
Letter of Marque

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:48.770
So what are they talking about?

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:51.100
He's reporting the ghost ship,

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:52.850
and asking them to investigate further.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:57.440
It's pretty far away.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.740
So it wasn't the Bentenmaru.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.740
He's done talking.

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:05.910
Wouldn't a pirate ship be too busy
to deal with some high schoolers?

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:07.950
You think so?

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:09.290
Feel better?

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:14.960
I wonder who made it possible
to bug the captain's room.

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:17.540
I think it was some upperclassmen,

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.720
wanting to know what the
teachers were saying.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:22.760
President,

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:24.260
I'd like to talk to everyone.

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:27.010
I thought you might.

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:42.530
An enemy masquerading as
a ghost ship is after us?

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:46.030
I think that's our best guess
at the moment.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:50.790
The communication problems we've
experienced since leaving port

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:53.910
are probably the work of the same group

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:56.210
that attacked us at the relay station.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.920
So they've even provided a villain

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.460
for our high school girls' cruise?

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:03.130
That's a nice bonus.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.970
Then we don't know who it is yet, right?

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:10.810
Not yet.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:13.560
But I know why they're pursuing us.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:16.730
Why is that?

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:20.020
Because I'm on board this ship.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:29.830
Candidate for captaincy of the
space pirate ship: Bentenmaru,

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:31.370
Kato Marika?

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:35.620
As club president,

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:38.290
I make it a point to know my members.

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:42.800
I'm looking forward to being able to see
some space piracy around here.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:44.970
Well, I'm still just a candidate.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:46.930
I haven't decided that I'm doing it.

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:48.890
Listen, everyone.

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:52.140
Because of some stuff with her family,
bad guys are after Marika.

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:54.600
Marika, what did you do?

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:58.520
Everyone here has their
own circumstances.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:03.610
It just happens you might
become a pirate captain.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:04.610
That's all.

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:06.280
Space pirates?

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:07.510
I didn't know they still existed.

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:08.990
Sorry.

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:09.950
They do.

00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:11.660
So it's like a tradition?

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:14.370
Leaving that aside...

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.790
Anyway, the enemy is after this ship.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:21.420
What do you think, Marika?

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:24.380
You've already considered it,
haven't you?

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:27.420
What should the Odette II do?

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:28.340
Call the military?

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:30.550
Or...

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.600
Let's fight them off ourselves!

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:41.770
Why do you say that?

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:45.070
If you're just trying to be cool,
I can't agree to it.

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:48.070
To keep the adults from trying anything

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:49.990
if the Odette II goes on
another practice cruise.

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:53.660
It's not for my sake.

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:56.200
It's for Hakuoh Academy's Yacht Club.

00:14:57.330 --> 00:14:59.290
Is that strange?

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:01.250
Can we do it?

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.710
With the Odette II and this crew,
I'm certain we can!

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:11.550
This is unexpected.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:12.970
I get it.

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:15.260
I get it. So eat breakfast,
and then get some sleep.

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.480
We'll take care of the rest
when you wake up.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:19.230
Yes!

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:32.660
Coming in.

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:33.950
Hey.

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:36.080
Is something up?

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:36.990
Nothing.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:40.290
We're almost to the
other side of Tau Ceti.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:42.960
There aren't ghost ships or anything.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:47.250
I'll be at dinner.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:50.130
Call me if there's an emergency.

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:52.130
Okay, bye.

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:54.390
Did you get some sleep?

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.430
I was making that.

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.680
It isn't done yet, but did you see it?

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:02.310
I did.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:05.020
A prediction for the enemy attack,
and a counterattack plan.

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:07.070
But can we predict how

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:11.490
the enemy will attack when we
don't know their location?

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:14.820
The Odette II will reach the maximum distance
from Sea of the Morningstar

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:17.990
as it approaches Sand of the Red Star,
on the other side of Tau Ceti.

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:22.040
If I were going to attack an unsuspecting
ship on a practice cruise,

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:25.170
I'd most likely hide in the
Sand of the Red Star's shadow,

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:26.710
and then attack from behind.

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:29.760
Attacking a ship of girls from behind...

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:31.510
How barbaric...

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:34.470
I suppose that's why they're villains.

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:37.010
There's a lot written down,

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:41.140
but this plan relies on convincing the
enemy that their own plot is going well.

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:45.270
They'll be preparing things before
making contact with us.

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:47.360
So we must pretend that
whatever they do works.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:48.690
That's strategy.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:49.110
I see.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:52.070
Even if we pretend they've fooled us,

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:56.160
what happens if their electronic warfare
really <i>does</i> take over our system?

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:59.490
Until now, their electronic attacks
have been quite conventional.

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:02.450
They don't appear to be particularly
technologically inclined.

00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:09.250
We'll completely duplicate the Odette II's
systems in an isolated area,

00:17:09.250 --> 00:17:12.210
and then reroute all their
cracking attempts to that.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:13.550
We'll let them do as they like to it,

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:15.800
and it won't affect our navigation.

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:18.180
We should be able to see
everything they do.

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:19.760
That's tactics.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:20.890
Well,

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:24.520
I don't really understand the difference
between strategy and tactics.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:26.060
Is that how it works?

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:27.230
Even if you don't understand,

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:29.020
as long as you can think
of them as separate things,

00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:30.020
that's good enough.

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:33.240
So, what does the vice-president say?

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.530
I sent it to her when I sent it to you.

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:37.860
I think she's looking at it right now.

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:38.370
Lynn?

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:39.490
Saw it.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:40.700
What do you think?

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:42.330
I know the net,

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:46.000
but electronic warfare between two ships
in flight falls outside my specialty.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:48.460
I'm just a high school girl, you know.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:51.920
And the electronic weaponry on this ship...

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:55.420
I still don't understand a lot of it.

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:57.090
It's rare

00:17:57.090 --> 00:18:00.220
for you to be at a loss with computers.

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.350
Well, the basics of electronic warfare

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:06.020
don't change much so long as you're
still using EM waves and networks.

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:08.270
We've managed to deal

00:18:08.270 --> 00:18:11.520
with everything they've
thrown at us so far.

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:15.030
And Kato's notes are a little vague,
but essentially accurate.

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:17.490
Sorry they're vague.

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:20.950
You think it'll work?

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:23.370
If we prepare, have our
equipment ready,

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:25.830
and make the right choices,
we can do anything.

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:27.460
What do you think, President?

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:29.750
I'm sure you already knew,

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.210
but this ship used to be a pirate ship.

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:34.090
You might say it's a veteran.

00:18:34.090 --> 00:18:35.130
Then, President...

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:36.630
Marika,

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:39.720
I want you to consider what will happen
if things get messier.

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:43.600
Think of how to prepare everyone
for every possible outcome.

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:45.100
Roger.

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:48.270
All that's left is telling everyone else to

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:49.850
prepare to fight this ghost ship.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.610
That seems to be the plan.

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:54.650
Impressive, huh?

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:56.940
They know we're on board,

00:18:56.940 --> 00:18:59.860
and they're secretly planning
a battle anyway.

00:18:59.860 --> 00:19:04.120
And all of them know who Marika is.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.830
They're just enjoying themselves.

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:09.290
They each have their own circumstances.

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:11.080
The president is an extremely
rich tycoon's daughter.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:14.420
Her future husband has been selected
for her since she was little.
Jenny Dolittle

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:14.670
Lynn Lambretta

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:18.010
In middle school, the vice-president was
placed on probation for cracking.

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:18.720
Asta Alhanko

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:21.840
All of them have other interesting stories.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:23.720
So don't you think they'd understand?

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:26.640
Then I guess the yacht club is

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:28.430
the right place for her.

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:30.390
Still, they're all good kids.

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:31.850
But this plan she wrote...

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:34.020
Part of it depends on luck,

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:36.110
and some of it's just irrelevant.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:38.280
It has all kinds of problems.

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:40.950
But the premise is valid.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:43.820
I think it's pretty good,
for a novice's first attempt.

00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:45.120
Pretty good?

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:46.490
Don't be silly.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:48.330
It's amazing.

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:50.160
She came up with this

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:52.750
on zero sleep and without experience.

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:54.750
In a few years, she'll be terrifying.

00:19:54.750 --> 00:19:56.380
So what will you do?

00:19:57.130 --> 00:19:59.130
Marika has made her decision.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:04.050
As club advisor, my only option is feigning
ignorance until the very end.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:04.720
I'll help 'em out.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:07.220
They're <i>my</i> students.

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:09.180
I have to make sure they get home safe.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:11.140
Good luck, Mr. Kane.

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:12.770
I see them.

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:15.610
700,000 km, on the side facing the sun.

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:18.480
It's a miniscule reading, but this is probably it.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:19.940
What is it?

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:21.780
From this distance and at this size,

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:24.070
it's impossible to get detailed specs.

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:27.530
But I think it's an ultra-small,
unmanned stealth unit.

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:32.330
Then its largest dimension would
be less than a meter?

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:34.620
I'm amazed you found something that tiny.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.840
I scanned with the sun sensor.

00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:42.050
That's what the ghost ship really is?

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:46.640
Scan with a high-frequency radar,
and it pretends to be the battleship Alcyon.

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:49.560
If you recreate a ship's hull from the plans,

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:52.350
you can fake a radar signature.

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:54.850
Mix in something to compensate
for the Doppler effect,

00:20:54.850 --> 00:20:58.610
add some noise, return it to
the source of the radar,

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:00.320
and you have yourself a ghost ship.

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:02.360
Convenient.

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:03.320
So what do we do?

00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:06.700
Let's pretend we haven't figured
it out, and we know nothing.

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:07.530
But...

00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:08.740
But?

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:11.040
This fake ghost ship will probably act

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:13.500
as the antenna for their
electronic attacks.

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:17.460
If possible, I'd like to deploy it against
the enemy mother ship.

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:20.750
Yeah, it'd be nice if we could...

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:22.710
Take over the ghost ship?

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:23.970
If we could.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:31.180
If you can't do it, Lynn, just say so.

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:32.470
No, I'll try.

00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:35.690
President, can I connect my
computer to the ship's systems?

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:38.150
Don't ruin the systems.

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:41.150
And don't put a lot of extra
shortcuts everywhere!

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:45.700
Jeez...

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:47.110
Also, President,

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:48.110
What?

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:51.080
I'd like to change the flight plan a little.

00:21:57.790 --> 00:22:00.340
It's going to be a long night...

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.300
C.M.S. C.M.A. O.P.R. Into the space

00:22:08.300 --> 00:22:11.600
The Final Battle is at Midnight
C.M.S. C.M.A. O.P.R. Into the space

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:12.510
C.M.S. C.M.A. O.P.R. Into the space

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:18.020
M.C.Z. Overall O.I.E. What's goin' on?

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:21.020
Welcome glory, let's breakthrough and fly out now, let's go!

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:24.400
Run towards an unlimited dimension. Come, let's accelerate beyond this

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:27.490
history that's to be known, regardless of time. Fly towards a destined future.

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:30.530
Just a fossil if you give up a miracle. Your decision is your best choice.

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:39.460
There's another, unknown, me hiding in my heart.

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:44.340
M.C.Z. Overall O.I.E. What's goin' on?

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:48.170
How far?

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:50.840
Go, go, go, go, ring a bell! Go, go, ring, ring a bell!

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:54.470
Until the end.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:55.810
Go, go, go, go, ring a bell!

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:59.140
You're lost and missing.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:07.990
We're lost without a hope of any shelter.

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:11.740
But why? What did we do? What did we do?

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:13.160
What should we do?

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:16.330
From the world beyond shadows invade.

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:20.620
We're destined to stop them with our lives.

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:26.670
So no matter where we go, as far as it takes

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:30.930
we'll always believe in ourselves.

00:23:39.350 --> 00:23:42.020
A mysterious, armed vessel lies in wait.

00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:44.310
Do the yacht club's frail members
have any chance to win?

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:47.780
They use all their weapons
to the best of their abilities,

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:50.490
and their resolve opens the way.

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:52.490
What is it Marika sees then?

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:55.660
Next time, on <i>Bodacious Space Pirates:</i>

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:57.040
Marika makes a decision.

00:23:57.580 --> 00:23:59.000
Look forward to it.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:05.000
Marika Makes a Decision

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:10.800
Breakfast beats worrying.
